<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelmstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelmstein is an artificial island fortress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steinhuder-meer">Steinhuder Meer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built between 1761 and 1765 by Count William of Schaumburg-Lippe. Designed to defend his small state, it also housed his military school, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> was trained. It withstood a siege by Hessian troops in 1787 and is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelmstein.jpg" alt="Wilhelmstein" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ChristianSchd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelmstein_island_Steinhuder_Meer_lake_Lower_Saxony_Germany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
